WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.399
In this film you check a FundsXML delivery in two stages: against the schema, and against the rules of the business.

2
00:00:07.449 --> 00:00:10.335
And you learn to read the typical error messages.

3
00:00:10.385 --> 00:00:11.952
It takes about six minutes.

4
00:00:13.202 --> 00:00:15.187
Validation runs in two stages.

5
00:00:15.237 --> 00:00:19.867
Stage one is the schema: structure, data types, code lists and links.

6
00:00:19.917 --> 00:00:25.903
It is fast, standard, and every tool can do it, as long as it supports XSD one point one.

7
00:00:25.953 --> 00:00:30.787
Stage two are business rules: sums, check digits, dates and consistency.

8
00:00:30.837 --> 00:00:35.942
They are written in a rule language such as Schematron, or built into tools.

9
00:00:35.992 --> 00:00:39.265
A file should pass both stages before it is sent.

10
00:00:40.219 --> 00:00:46.859
A fund administrator has sent this file: the month-end data of a fictional global equity fund.

11
00:00:46.909 --> 00:00:52.695
The root element is FundsXML4, and the first lines name the schema, FundsXML4.xsd.

12
00:00:52.745 --> 00:00:55.897
So FreeXmlToolkit can validate it right away.

13
00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:57.197
Press F8.

14
00:00:57.247 --> 00:01:01.253
The schema checks the structure and the data types of every element.

15
00:01:01.303 --> 00:01:04.296
The Problems panel below the editor lists an error.

16
00:01:04.946 --> 00:01:05.946
Click the problem.

17
00:01:05.996 --> 00:01:09.329
The caret jumps to the market value of the first position.

18
00:01:09.379 --> 00:01:13.762
An amount must be a decimal number, but someone has written it out in words.

19
00:01:14.413 --> 00:01:17.746
Type the exact number instead, and press F8 again.

20
00:01:17.796 --> 00:01:20.791
The error is gone, and the document is valid.

21
00:01:20.841 --> 00:01:26.746
Other deliveries may also show hints, in blue: suggestions for data that could be added.

22
00:01:26.796 --> 00:01:29.183
Hints are suggestions, not mistakes.

23
00:01:30.332 --> 00:01:34.136
Schema errors come in a few typical forms, and their codes help.

24
00:01:34.186 --> 00:01:42.326
Complex type two point four means an element is missing, unexpected, or in the wrong order; the message lists what was expected instead.

25
00:01:42.376 --> 00:01:47.565
Datatype errors mean a value has the wrong type, like the amount in words you just saw.

26
00:01:47.615 --> 00:01:50.576
Enumeration errors mean a code is not in its list.

27
00:01:50.626 --> 00:01:55.213
And an IDREF error means a position points to a security that does not exist.

28
00:01:56.563 --> 00:01:58.854
A message tells you more than its code.

29
00:01:58.904 --> 00:02:03.236
It names the line and the column, and the element where the check failed.

30
00:02:03.286 --> 00:02:09.599
Often it lists what the schema expected instead, for example the names of the elements that may come next.

31
00:02:09.649 --> 00:02:14.821
Read that list first: it usually points directly to a missing element or a wrong order.

32
00:02:14.871 --> 00:02:20.283
And fix the first error first, because one mistake can cause several messages further down.

33
00:02:21.533 --> 00:02:23.906
Now a second delivery of the same fund.

34
00:02:23.956 --> 00:02:26.639
It passes the schema without a single error.

35
00:02:26.689 --> 00:02:30.117
Every element is in its place, every number is a number.

36
00:02:30.769 --> 00:02:33.051
But the Problems panel shows two warnings.

37
00:02:33.101 --> 00:02:39.657
They come from the FundsXML data-quality checks: the weights of the positions add up to one hundred and twenty percent.

38
00:02:40.669 --> 00:02:43.139
Click the warning about the first position.

39
00:02:43.189 --> 00:02:49.762
It claims twenty point four percent, but its value is only zero point four percent of the fund's net asset value.

40
00:02:49.812 --> 00:02:51.989
Correct the weight and validate again.

41
00:02:52.039 --> 00:02:53.338
Both warnings are gone.

42
00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:58.840
The green FundsXML mark in the status bar shows that the document was recognised.

43
00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:07.675
With it come more than fifty checks: ISIN and LEI check digits, currency codes, dates, position values against the net asset value, and many more.

44
00:03:07.725 --> 00:03:10.640
A schema tells you whether a file is well formed.

45
00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.786
These checks tell you whether its data can be right.

46
00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:16.999
The checks have three levels.

47
00:03:17.049 --> 00:03:21.352
An error means the data cannot be right, for example a wrong check digit.

48
00:03:21.402 --> 00:03:26.181
A warning means it is probably wrong, like weights far above one hundred percent.

49
00:03:26.231 --> 00:03:31.249
And an info is a hint, for example that derivatives could also report their exposure.

50
00:03:31.299 --> 00:03:37.449
Agree with your partners which levels stop a delivery: usually errors always, and warnings after a look.

51
00:03:38.699 --> 00:03:40.918
For a full overview, use the report.

52
00:03:40.968 --> 00:03:44.259
In the FundsXML panel, click Run Data Quality Checks.

53
00:03:44.309 --> 00:03:49.742
A report opens next to the document: every check, whether it passed, and what it found.

54
00:03:49.792 --> 00:03:55.919
Save it with the delivery, or send it to your partner, so both sides talk about the same findings.

55
00:03:57.116 --> 00:04:04.190
The checks in FreeXmlToolkit are queries in XQuery, and you can open each of them as a snippet in the Query Console.

56
00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:13.279
For rules of your own, Schematron is the standard language: rules on XML, readable and testable, and they run in tools, scripts and pipelines alike.

57
00:04:13.329 --> 00:04:17.640
Add the fields a particular partner requires, or your house tolerances.

58
00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:25.992
FreeXmlToolkit runs Schematron too, and offers the quick fixes a Schematron file defines, so a common error is corrected with one click.

59
00:04:27.342 --> 00:04:28.544
Finally, automate it.

60
00:04:28.594 --> 00:04:34.688
On the command line, Saxon or Xerces validate files against the schema; both support XSD one point one.

61
00:04:34.738 --> 00:04:42.441
Run the schema check and your Schematron rules in every build and before every delivery, and stop the pipeline when a check fails.

62
00:04:42.491 --> 00:04:49.241
Keep a folder of test files, good and bad ones, and run them with every change of your mapping or of the standard.

63
00:04:50.591 --> 00:04:52.848
That was validation in two stages.

64
00:04:52.898 --> 00:04:59.838
In the next film, you query a FundsXML file, transform it, and see how it fits into a system landscape.

65
00:04:59.888 --> 00:05:01.195
Thanks for watching.
